INT. LAUNDROMAT - NIGHT

Fluorescent light, a dozen washers turning slow, lukewarm steam on the windows. Four people wait out their cycles.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
This is the last time they will all be in the same room, though none of them knows it yet.

LORETTA
(folding a pillowcase, humming)
My mother said never wash a dead man's shirts. I said, Ma, they're only shirts.

DEV
(not looking up from his phone, thumbing a message)
Cheap soap makes more suds. Doesn't make it clean.

PRIYA
(holding a hot towel to her cheek, watching the door)
Who's the guy outside? He's been standing by the bins for twenty minutes.

OSWALD
(stirring a paper cup of coffee, shrugging)
Waiting on a dryer, probably. Everybody waits on something.

The bell over the door jingles. A TALL MAN IN A DRIPPING YELLOW SLICKER steps in, carrying a laundry bag that is much too heavy and much too still.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Calm lasts exactly as long as the first drip hitting the tile.

THE TALL MAN
(setting the bag down gently, smiling)
Evening. Any machines free? I've a bit of a mess to wash.

The bag shifts. Nobody says a word. Every washer in the room stops spinning at once.
